faqs:
  - q: "How are measurement conversions calculated accurately?"
    a: "Non-temperature conversions normalize the source value to a fundamental SI base unit (like meters for length or kilograms for weight) using international NIST definitions, then divide by the target unit's exact conversion factor."
  - q: "What is the formula to convert Celsius to Fahrenheit?"
    a: "Multiply the Celsius temperature by 9/5 (or 1.8) and add 32: °F = (°C × 9/5) + 32. For example, 20°C converts to (20 × 1.8) + 32 = 68°F."
  - q: "What is the formula to convert Fahrenheit to Celsius?"
    a: "Subtract 32 from the Fahrenheit temperature, then multiply by 5/9: °C = (°F - 32) × 5/9. For instance, 98.6°F converts to (98.6 - 32) × 5/9 = 37°C."
  - q: "What is the difference between a US gallon and an Imperial gallon?"
    a: "A US liquid gallon is defined as exactly 231 cubic inches or approximately 3.78541 liters. An Imperial (British) gallon is defined as exactly 4.54609 liters, making the Imperial gallon approximately 20% larger than the US gallon."
  - q: "Are international conversion factors exact?"
    a: "Yes. By international agreement (the 1959 International Yard and Pound Agreement), 1 inch is defined as exactly 0.0254 meters, and 1 avoirdupois pound is defined as exactly 0.45359237 kilograms."
  - q: "How many decimal places of precision are displayed?"
    a: "Our unit converter displays high-precision floating-point values rounded to 6 decimal places for everyday physical units and 4 decimal places for temperatures, eliminating floating-point rounding errors."
  - q: "Are any measurements or user inputs stored or uploaded?"
    a: "No. All unit transformations occur entirely in your local browser using client-side JavaScript. No data is ever transmitted or logged."
