      <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Unit Conversion Pitfalls</h2>
      <ul style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.8; margin-left: 1.25rem;">
        <li><strong>Confusing US Liquid and Imperial Gallons:</strong> A British (Imperial) gallon is 4.546 liters, while a US gallon is only 3.785 liters. Using the wrong gallon definition leads to a 20% measurement discrepancy.</li>
        <li><strong>Applying Direct Factors to Temperatures:</strong> You cannot multiply Fahrenheit by a single ratio because Fahrenheit and Celsius have different zero points (freezing is 32°F and 0°C). Always subtract 32 before scaling.</li>
        <li><strong>Squaring Linear Conversion Factors for Area:</strong> Since 1 yard = 3 feet, 1 square yard is not 3 square feet, but <code>3 × 3 = 9</code> square feet. Area factors square the linear relationship.</li>
